<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  The scene life cycle, which iOS 26 and later require.

	  Without any manifest UIKit kills the app on launch — not a warning but an
	  immediate trap:

	      EXC_BREAKPOINT (SIGTRAP) in
	      ___UIApplicationEvaluateRuntimeIssueForNoSceneLifecycleAdoption_block_

	  which on a phone looks like a black flash and a return to the home screen,
	  with a crash report and nothing in the app's own log. The simulator did not
	  complain because the runtime installed at the time was iOS 18 — pick a
	  simulator by its *OS*, not by its device name, when a failure is
	  OS-dependent. An iPad Air on iPadOS 27.0 reproduces this one, and the
	  release-build crash that replaced it.

	  What `tao` does with this has changed, so the reasoning that used to be here
	  no longer applies. On `tao` 0.35 the *value* of
	  `UIApplicationSupportsMultipleScenes` was the switch that put tao into scene
	  mode at all (`multiple_scenes_enabled()`); on 0.36 and later that function is
	  gone. Tao now decides by the **presence of this manifest**
	  (`scene_lifecycle_enabled()`, `platform_impl/ios/scene.rs`) and registers
	  `application:configurationForConnectingSceneSession:options:` unconditionally,
	  so the delegate is installed whether the key is true or false.

	  That leaves the key meaning what UIKit says it means — "this app supports
	  more than one window" — and this app is single-window. It is kept **true**
	  here only because that is what has shipped and what was reviewed; `false` is
	  the more honest declaration and is now viable (`tao#1308` is what made it so),
	  but it changes windowing behaviour and wants its own tested change rather
	  than a ride along with a dependency upgrade.

	  There is deliberately no `UISceneConfigurations` here either. Tao builds the
	  configuration from the connecting session's own role with a `nil` name, which
	  selects the app's configuration for that role if one is declared and a
	  default otherwise; declaring one here would be a second, competing source of
	  truth. This is also why the old UIKit log line

	      Info.plist contained no UIScene configuration dictionary
	      (looking for configuration named "TaoScene")

	  no longer appears: `tao` 0.35 asked for a configuration by that name and
	  0.36+ does not.

	  Tauri's iOS template does not add this file. Tauri merges it over the
	  generated `Info.plist` **at build time** — `tauri ios init` alone does not,
	  so the built app's plist is where to check it landed.
	-->
	<key>UIApplicationSceneManifest</key>
	<dict>
		<key>UIApplicationSupportsMultipleScenes</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>

	<!--
	  Tone practice records from the microphone, and iOS terminates an app that
	  asks for one without saying why. The string is shown verbatim in the
	  permission prompt, so it says what happens rather than what is wanted:
	  the audio is scored on the device and nothing is kept or sent.
	-->
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Hanzi Tutor listens while you hold the button, to check the tone of what you said. The recording is scored on this device and is never saved or sent anywhere.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
